</form><html xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:math="java.lang.Math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:fo="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Format" xmlns:fn="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-functions" xmlns:xdt="http://www.w3.org/2005/02/xpath-datatypes" xmlns:caml="http://lc.ca.gov/legalservices/schemas/caml.1#" xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>HR132:v99#DOCUMENT</title><meta name="dc:identifier" content="HR132"/><meta name="dc:title" content="HR &#xA;              132-&#xA;                INTRODUCED&#xA;              "/><meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html;charset=utf-8"/></head><body><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Start</h2></span><div id="bill_all" align="justify"><div id="about"><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td align="center"/></tr></tbody></table></div><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><font size="3" style="FONT-FAMILY: MinionPC, Arial Unicode MS">
                    CALIFORNIA LEGISLATURE—
                    2025–2026 REGULAR SESSION</font></div><br clear="all"/><span id="bill_house_num"><div style="float: left; width:50%; font-weight: bold; "><span style="text-transform: uppercase; text-align:left;width:50%;"><b>
                House Resolution
              </b></span></div><div style="float:right; width:50%; text-align:right;"><span style="text-transform: uppercase;"><b>No. 132</b></span></div></span><hr/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><table style="border-collapse: collapse" cellSpacing="0" align="center" role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td id="bill_authors" align="center"><span style="font-weight:bold;">Introduced by Assembly Member Bauer-Kahan<br/></span></td></tr><tr><td id="bill_intro_date" align="center" class="textcenter"><br/>August 04, 2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div><hr/><br clear="all"/><div id="title"><span style="font-weight:normal;font-size:0.9em;"> Relative to Young Women’s Reproductive Health Awareness Month.</span></div><br clear="all"/><br clear="all"/><div align="center"><h2>LEGISLATIVE COUNSEL'S DIGEST</h2></div><br/><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0"><span id="subject">HR 132, as introduced, Bauer-Kahan.
                          </span></div><div><span id="digesttext"/></div><span class="hidden"><h2>Digest Key</h2></span></div><hr/><span class="hidden"><h2>Bill Text</h2></span><span class="Resolution" title="source: /caml:MeasureDoc/caml:Resolution&#xA;    "><span class="Whereas" id="id_D4D3E35F-6509-4E59-8E2B-D038D29FB4A1"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Adolescence is a critical stage in physical and emotional development, during which young women begin making important decisions about their health and well-being; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_6A15F7FE-E6A1-46FA-AA7A-AD48AD62D4E3"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Many young women lack awareness and timely diagnosis of reproductive conditions and diseases, including endometriosis, which can cause menstrual pain, fertility issues, and long-term health consequences if untreated; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_B7BCCCFA-FD3A-4558-916D-FBC7603A6324"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Menstrual health is a critical, but often overlooked, component of teen health, and untreated menstrual disorders can lead to lifelong reproductive complications if not addressed early; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_C60DFBCC-E85D-430F-98E1-0D403FA1AD4B"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Black women are 50 percent less likely to be diagnosed with endometriosis than White women, and therefore less likely to get treatment, often due to systemic barriers and stigma surrounding the issue; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_4065E572-CC8F-482C-8235-FDBBD4503F67"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Comprehensive education on safe sexual health practices, including, but not limited to, prevention of sexually transmitted infections (STIs), contraceptives, and consent, is vital for supporting the emotional health and well-being of teen girls; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_0A208A6F-132A-456D-A326-C48500ED12F8"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, LGBTQ+ youth may experience stigma, discrimination, or exclusion in health care settings, creating further inequities in access to reproductive and sexual health services; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_0D6DF4EB-0CE0-48B1-A4F6-B6DE435F65AC"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Cultural stigma, lack of culturally appropriate resources, and language barriers can prevent immigrant and refugee teens from seeking timely reproductive health information and care; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_53C38F93-415A-4D22-9AC9-B98160D2CA55"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, The human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine is a proven, safe, and effective tool to prevent cervical, anal, oral, and other cancers, yet vaccination rates among teens in California remain below public health targets; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_03E0C64F-056B-49F5-9ECB-6E743506AFB6"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, Hispanic women in the United States have some of the lowest HPV vaccination rates and the highest rates of cervical cancer compared to other populations. These outcomes are rooted in structural inequities surrounding access to care; and</div></span><span class="Whereas" id="id_8D480985-354C-44A8-B601-0C5929B59C70"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">WHEREAS, California has long been a leader in reproductive rights and promoting medically accurate health education; now, therefore, be it</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_CB46C949-2871-4177-8748-88C0628A45D2"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved by the Assembly of the State of California, That the Assembly proclaims the month of September 2026 as Young Women’s Reproductive Health Awareness Month; and be it further</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_69B53242-E512-48F7-9E75-5428D612A070"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved, That the Assembly encourages high schools, colleges, public health agencies, pediatricians, and community health providers to promote educational campaigns on reproductive health topics relevant to teenage girls, including menstrual health conditions, HPV vaccination, and healthy sexual health practices; and be it further</div></span><span class="Resolved" id="id_F0DA1206-B40E-463F-89F6-947788FDAC0C"><div style="margin:0 0 1em 0;">Resolved, That the Chief Clerk of the Assembly transmit copies of this resolution to the author for appropriate distribution.</div></span></span></div></body></html>
